Does thermal throttling move the clock when the clocks are locked?
==================================================================

Device   DN-ENG41 (GigglePal_Companion), Android 14 / API 34, serial T3515NNFBC000055
Date     2026-10-31
Commit   38137301f
Question Section 10.6 reads a thermal warning through on this device on the argument
         that the measured effect floor already contains whatever the throttling costs.
         This tests the stronger claim directly: with the clocks pinned, does the
         thermal governor move the CPU frequency at all?

Setup
-----
  adb root; ./gradlew :benchmark-android:lockClocks; adb shell svc power stayon true

lockClocks pinned the cores with the userspace governor, scaling_min == scaling_max:

  cpu0-3 (little)  1190400 kHz   of cpuinfo_max 1900800
  cpu4-7 (big)     1344000 kHz   of cpuinfo_max 2400000   (56% of maximum)

IMPORTANT: lockClocks also tries to stop the thermal daemon, and this device REFUSED:

  Unable to stop service 'perfd'
  Unable to stop service 'vendor.thermal-engine'
  Unable to stop service 'vendor.perfd'
  Failed to set property 'ctl.interface_restart' to 'android.hardware.power@1.0::IPower/default'

So vendor.thermal-engine was running for the whole measurement. Nothing here suppresses
thermal management; that is deliberate, and BENCHMARKS.md section 6 rules it out anyway.
"Unable to lock memory bus" -- the bus is not pinned, so this test covers CPU frequency
and nothing else.

Workload
--------
  ./gradlew :benchmark-android:connectedReleaseAndroidTest -Pl2.matrix=ci -Pl2.throttleWait=0
  BUILD SUCCESSFUL in 5m 9s

Thermal state throughout: Thermal Status: 3 (SEVERE). It never left it.

Evidence 1 -- kernel cpufreq residency, counters reset immediately before the run
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpuN/cpufreq/stats/time_in_state, in 10 ms ticks.

  cpu4 (big)                        cpu0 (little)
   300000        0                   300000        0
   806400        0                   691200        0
  1056000        0                   940800        0
  1344000    33425   <- locked      1190400    33438   <- locked
  1766400        0                  1516800        0
  2208000        0                  1804800        0
  2400000        0                  1900800        0

334.25 s on the big cores and 334.38 s on the little cores, 100% of it at the locked
frequency. Zero ticks at every other frequency -- including every frequency BELOW the
locked one, which is where a thermal mitigation would have put it.

Evidence 2 -- 1 Hz sampling during the run, 175 samples
--------------------------------------------------------
Every one of the 175 samples, without exception:

  status=3  cpu0=1190400/1190400  cpu4=1344000/1344000

scaling_cur_freq never moved, and scaling_max_freq was never clamped: a thermal cooling
device lowering the policy ceiling would have shown up in the second number.

Conclusion
----------
Across 5 minutes 9 seconds at thermal status SEVERE with the vendor thermal daemon
running, thermal throttling moved the CPU frequency ZERO times.

With the cores pinned at 56% of maximum, the operating point is already below anything
the thermal governor would reduce it to, so there is nothing for it to take away. On this
device, in this protocol, THERMAL_THROTTLE is a label on the run rather than an effect in
the numbers.

What this does NOT show
-----------------------
- Only CPU frequency. The memory bus could not be locked, and the GPU is not exercised by
  L2. A thermal effect through either would not appear here.
- One run of 5 minutes. A longer soak (L4, section 12) could still find something; that is
  one of the reasons L4 exists.
- Nothing about absolute speed. These timings belong to a warm device at a pinned, reduced
  operating point, and section 6's rule that absolute numbers belong to their operating
  point is unchanged.

